# ============================================================================
# WHITELIST-FIRST .gitignore
# ============================================================================
# Nothing is tracked, staged or pushed unless it is un-ignored on purpose.
# A deny-list would make every new file tracked by default and depend on
# someone remembering; this structure fails closed instead.
#
# Rules that decide whether this file is correct (all fail silently):
#   1. `*` matches a path but NOT what is inside a directory tree, so every
#      versioned tree needs the pair `!dir/` + `!dir/**`.
#   2. Ties resolve by LAST match, so re-ignores must come AFTER un-ignores.
#   3. `*` also ignores `.gitignore` itself, so it is un-ignored explicitly.
#   4. A trailing `# ...` is part of the pattern, not a comment. Comments are
#      on their own lines only.
#   5. Anchor root-level trees with a leading slash (`!/docs/`), otherwise the
#      name matches at every level.
# ============================================================================


# --- 1. Ignore everything by default ---------------------------------------
*


# --- 2. Whitelist: root-level files ---------------------------------------
!.gitignore
!LICENSE
!README.md


# --- 3. Whitelist: shipped source and documentation trees ------------------
!/.github/
!/.github/**
!/docs/
!/docs/**
!/scripts/
!/scripts/**
!/skills/
!/skills/**


# --- 4. Re-ignore what the un-ignores swept back in ------------------------
# Must stay after section 3 - ties go to the last match.
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]


# --- 5. Never tracked, by design -------------------------------------------
# No un-ignore rule exists for any of these, so the leading `*` keeps them out
# permanently - there is nothing to forget later. Recorded for intent only.
#
#   *.log    helper run logs
#   .env     real credentials - never versioned
#   Thumbs.db  .DS_Store  OS noise


# Verify before trusting this file:
#   git add -A --dry-run                 lists exactly what would be staged
#   git check-ignore -v data/x.json      must resolve to the leading `*`
#   git ls-files -ci --exclude-standard  must be empty
